{"prompt": {"messages": [{"role": "system", "content": "You are a helpful assistant that repairs Python 3 code so that it correctly solves the given programming problem. The code reads and writes I/O from the console."}, {"role": "user", "content": "Repair the following Python 3 code so that it correctly solves the programming problem. The code reads and writes I/O from the console. Your repaired code should implement the problem correctly, meaning that it should produce the correct output for any valid input.\nPrint your repaired code and also the confidence score you give to your repair. The confidence score should be a number between 0 and 1. The format of the output should be:\n<repaired_code>\n<your_repaired_code>\n</repaired_code>\n<confidence>\n<your_confidence_score>\n</confidence>\nDo not provide any other text or markdown fences.\n\nHere is the problem description:\n<problem_description>\nSometimes one has to spell email addresses over the phone. Then one usually pronounces a dot as dot, an at sign as at. As a result, we get something like vasyaatgmaildotcom. Your task is to transform it into a proper email address (vasya@gmail.com). It is known that a proper email address contains only such symbols as . @ and lower-case Latin letters, doesn't start with and doesn't end with a dot. Also, a proper email address doesn't start with and doesn't end with an at sign. Moreover, an email address contains exactly one such symbol as @, yet may contain any number (possible, zero) of dots. You have to carry out a series of replacements so that the length of the result was as short as possible and it was a proper email address. If the lengths are equal, you should print the lexicographically minimal result. Overall, two variants of replacement are possible: dot can be replaced by a dot, at can be replaced by an at. \n</problem_description>\n\nHere is the Python 3 code you have to repair:\n<original_code>\n<your_rewritten_code_3>\nimport sys\ndata = sys.stdin.readline().rstrip('\\n')\nsize = len(data)\nstep1 = data[0]\nstep2 = data[1:size-3].replace('dot', '.')\nstep3 = data[size-3:]\ntemp = step1 + step2 + step3\npos = temp.find('at', 1)\noutput = temp[:pos] + '@' + temp[pos+2:]\nprint(output)\n</your_rewritten_code_3>\n</original_code>\n"}], "temp": 0, "sample_size": 1, "logprobs": true, "model": "qwen/qwen3-coder-next"}, "response": {"samples": [{"content": "<repaired_code>\nimport sys\ndata = sys.stdin.readline().rstrip('\\n')\n\n# We need to find all occurrences of \"dot\" and \"at\" and decide how to replace them\n# The key insight: we need exactly one '@' and any number of dots\n# We should minimize length (so replace \"dot\" with \".\" and \"at\" with \"@\")\n# But we need to ensure the result is a valid email address\n\n# Strategy: try all possible positions for the '@' symbol (must be where \"at\" appears)\n# For each candidate '@' position, replace all other \"dot\" with '.' and ensure validity\n\ncandidates = []\n\n# Find all positions where \"at\" occurs\nat_positions = []\ni = 0\nwhile i < len(data) - 1:\n    if data[i:i+2] == 'at':\n        at_positions.append(i)\n        i += 2\n    else:\n        i += 1\n\n# Also consider that \"at\" might be part of \"dot\" - but \"dot\" doesn't contain \"at\"\n# So we only look for standalone \"at\"\n\nfor at_pos in at_positions:\n    # Build candidate email\n    # Replace all \"dot\" with '.' except we need to be careful about overlapping\n    # First, create a version where we replace \"at\" at position at_pos with '@'\n    # and replace all \"dot\" with '.'\n    \n    # We'll build the string by scanning through\n    result = []\n    i = 0\n    while i < len(data):\n        if i == at_pos:\n            # This is where we put '@'\n            result.append('@')\n            i += 2  # skip 'at'\n        elif i < len(data) - 2 and data[i:i+3] == 'dot':\n            result.append('.')\n            i += 3\n        else:\n            result.append(data[i])\n            i += 1\n    \n    candidate = ''.join(result)\n    \n    # Validate candidate\n    if '@' not in candidate:\n        continue\n    at_count = candidate.count('@')\n    if at_count != 1:\n        continue\n    \n    # Check it doesn't start or end with '.' or '@'\n    if candidate[0] == '.' or candidate[0] == '@' or candidate[-1] == '.' or candidate[-1] == '@':\n        continue\n    \n    # Check all characters are valid (lowercase letters, '.', '@')\n    valid_chars = set('abcdefghijklmnopqrstuvwxyz.@')\n    if not all(c in valid_chars for c in candidate):\n        continue\n    \n    # Check no consecutive dots (not explicitly required but good practice)\n    # Actually the problem doesn't forbid consecutive dots, so we don't need this check\n    \n    candidates.append(candidate)\n\n# If no candidates found, try the original approach\nif not candidates:\n    # Fallback: replace first \"at\" with '@' and all \"dot\" with '.'\n    temp = data.replace('dot', '.')\n    pos = temp.find('at')\n    if pos != -1:\n        output = temp[:pos] + '@' + temp[pos+2:]\n        candidates.append(output)\n\n# Choose the shortest, then lexicographically smallest\nif candidates:\n    candidates.sort(key=lambda x: (len(x), x))\n    print(candidates[0])\nelse:\n    # This shouldn't happen with valid input, but just in case\n    print(data)\n</repaired_code>\n<confidence>\n0.7\n</confidence>", "token_logprobs": [-5.960462772236497e-07, -0.0009810879128053784, -1.1920922133867862e-06, -5.960462772236497e-07, -0.2043907642364502, -0.002811647718772292, -0.07889305055141449, -0.019925199449062347, 0.0, -1.1920922133867862e-06, -8.22540732769994e-06, -4.172316494077677e-06, -3.576278118089249e-07, -0.0005031750188209116, -4.2199197196168825e-05, -4.410734163684538e-06, -0.4741208553314209, -0.0004702892620116472, -0.9127627611160278, -0.0010082405060529709, -4.768370445162873e-07, -0.13105763494968414, -0.24583955109119415, -0.007899354211986065, -1.7881233361549675e-05, -0.14267674088478088, -0.089